+# Caller-scoped memory and meeting access authority
+
+**Issue:** `bdd55b7e-2acb-43a4-847d-55a2acb11571`
+**Classification:** Critical - authorization boundary and data migration
+**Stage:** Plan review
+**Date:** 2026-08-08
+**Base:** `origin/main@42e30d88bc516dc6472c9f1bb837bd694844aa47`
+
+## Outcome
+
+Make meeting-aware chat fail closed: the asking internal user may retrieve a
+meeting or any artifact derived from it only while that user has active access
+to the source meeting. Authorization is evaluated before content reaches a
+model, provider, tool result, prompt, memory injection, search result, or
+proposal response.
+
+This slice unlocks an omnipresent `search_meetings` capability without creating
+a second identity system. It reuses the product-owned collaboration actor,
+conversation, membership, event, run, and attribution contracts already on
+`main`.
+
+## Success criteria
+
+1. Every meeting has one canonical collaboration conversation as its access
+   origin. An active tenant-scoped conversation membership is the only group
+   grant.
+2. Meeting owner, verified internal participants, and explicit shares project
+   to conversation memberships. Free-form participant labels are display data
+   only and grant nothing.
+3. Meeting-derived memories, knowledge chunks, proposals, summaries, chapters,
+   topics, action items, and future artifacts retain the source conversation
+   ID and tenant. A write without a valid access origin fails atomically.
+4. All direct reads and indirect retrieval paths resolve the caller's internal
+   user to a stable active collaboration actor on the server and check current
+   membership. A client, model, provider, or connector cannot select the actor,
+   tenant, visibility, or membership.
+5. Missing actor, tenant mismatch, missing provenance, inactive membership,
+   revoked membership, deleted source, and malformed IDs return no content.
+   Direct-object endpoints use a uniform not-found response; searches return an
+   empty lane plus privacy-safe denial telemetry.
+6. Same-tenant non-members cannot discover the existence, metadata, text,
+   summaries, action items, derivative memories/chunks, or proposal rationale
+   of a restricted meeting.
+7. Existing `private` and `org` memory behavior remains supported, while the
+   currently tenant-only memory detail/list/chain APIs become caller-aware.
+8. A restricted derivative cannot become tenant-wide by accepting or copying
+   it. Publishing to the organization is a separate, explicit declassification
+   action requiring conversation-admin authority and an audit event.
+9. Tests prove positive, negative, cross-tenant, revocation, missing-origin,
+   indirect-laundering, and provider-spoofing cases against real query paths.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Provider-specific identity, ACL, RLS, or transport authority.
+- Public-link sharing, external guests, cross-tenant federation, calendar ACL
+  synchronization, email/label identity guessing, or provider participant
+  auto-linking.
+- Replacing Vercel AI SDK, the product-owned run protocol, collaboration
+  contracts, or current memory ranking.
+- Offline authorization decisions or caching positive permission checks across
+  requests. Offline clients may cache only previously authorized presentation
+  data and must reauthorize before sync/read.
+- Transcript-wide semantic indexing, global search, new ranking models, or a
+  generic policy language.
+- UI redesign, notification workflows, retention/redaction policy, or a
+  provider connector.
+- Making `team` or `public` meeting visibility a tenant-wide grant. Those legacy
+  values are compatibility metadata until an explicit migration decision is
+  made.
+
+## Current-state findings
+
+### Authority already available
+
+- `conversationContract` defines product-owned actors, active memberships,
+  reader/writer/admin roles, server-derived actor identity, and deny-by-default
+  behavior.
+- `authorizeConversation` checks tenant, active actor, active membership, and
+  role. This is the missing group resolver that previously prevented a third
+  memory visibility class.
+- `AgentRunContext.userId` is server-owned and already reaches memory retrieval.
+  Run and memory attribution tables exist; the run is not treated as an actor.
+
+### Gaps that permit leakage or laundering
+
+- Meetings store an owner and free-form `participant_labels`; their current
+  `team`/`public` rules are not backed by verified membership.
+- Memory injection handles `private` and `org`, but direct memory detail,
+  chain, and list functions are tenant-scoped and do not receive the caller.
+- Knowledge retrieval is tenant/scope-scoped and hard-pins memory results to
+  `org`; `search_knowledge` does not receive the asking user.
+- Meeting ingest creates tenant-wide proposals and copies meeting action text
+  into proposal rationale without carrying the source ACL.
+- Memories and knowledge chunks record source references but not a mandatory
+  authorization origin. Proposal rows also lack an access origin.
+- Revocation cannot consistently remove already-derived content because the
+  derivative is not joined back to a current membership check.
+
+## Selected design
+
+### 1. One access origin, not a second ACL vocabulary
+
+Each meeting is linked to exactly one collaboration conversation. The
+conversation is the stable access origin for the meeting and its derivatives.
+The meeting domain may retain legacy visibility fields for compatibility, but
+authorization code must not interpret them as grants.
+
+For the MVP:
+
+- Owner -> `admin` conversation membership.
+- Explicit internal share -> requested `reader`, `writer`, or `admin`
+  membership, subject to existing role rules.
+- Verified internal participant -> `reader` membership only when the product
+  already has an exact internal user ID in the tenant.
+- Free-form name, email, calendar label, speaker label, or provider identity ->
+  no membership and no grant.
+
+The server derives the caller from the authenticated internal user and resolves
+an active collaboration actor. Model/tool inputs never include `actorId`,
+`tenantId`, `conversationId`, visibility, or an authorization override.
+
+### 2. Provenance is authorization data
+
+Add the meeting conversation ID as the access origin to every content-bearing
+meeting derivative that can escape the meeting aggregate:
+
+| Data | Required representation |
+|---|---|
+| Meeting | `conversation_id` with same-tenant reference |
+| Meeting child tables | Authorize through `meeting_id -> conversation_id` |
+| Memory | `visibility = 'conversation'` plus `access_conversation_id` |
+| Knowledge chunk | nullable `access_conversation_id`; mandatory for meeting sources |
+| Proposal | nullable `access_conversation_id`; mandatory for meeting ingest |
+| Run/tool evidence | source IDs and decision outcome only; no denied content |
+
+Database checks enforce compatible combinations. New meeting-derived writes
+with no access conversation are rejected. Application writes copy provenance
+in the same transaction as the derivative so no briefly public row exists.
+
+Authorization is evaluated against current membership at read time. Do not
+cache a positive decision beyond the request or stream. Membership revocation
+therefore removes future access to the source and all derivatives without
+rewriting every derivative.
+
+### 3. Uniform caller-scoped read predicate
+
+All content-bearing queries use one shared semantic predicate:
+
+```text
+tenant matches
+AND caller resolves to an active internal collaboration actor
+AND (
+  content is caller-owned private content
+  OR content is explicitly tenant-wide org content
+  OR content has a valid access conversation and caller has active membership
+)
+```
+
+Meeting reads use only the third lane. Cross-tenant joins are prevented with
+composite tenant references and repeated tenant predicates. Caller identity is
+an input to the repository from authenticated server context, not request JSON.
+
+Direct-object reads return the same not-found response for missing and denied
+objects. Search and injection silently omit denied candidates, but record a
+structured reason code and source kind after authorization. They never log the
+denied title, transcript, text, embedding, summary, participant label, or
+proposal rationale.
+
+### 4. Safe meeting search for chat
+
+Add a product-owned `search_meetings` tool backed by the shared predicate.
+Inputs are query/filter/limit only. `userId`, tenant, run ID, and conversation
+context come from `AgentRunContext`.
+
+The first slice returns bounded meeting metadata, safe summaries, and action
+items only after membership authorization. Raw transcript passages are not
+returned by default. Every returned source is attributed to the run with its
+meeting/conversation origin and an allowed decision. A denied candidate is not
+made visible to the model, including through counts or error wording.
+
+### 5. Derivative propagation and declassification
+
+Meeting proposal ingest reads only meetings visible to the initiating caller
+and stamps the source access conversation on the proposal. Proposal list,
+detail, accept, reject, and audit paths repeat the current-membership check.
+
+Accepting a conversation-scoped proposal must preserve the source scope on the
+created work item or remain unavailable outside the source conversation. A
+tenant-wide destination is a distinct `publish_to_org` operation that requires:
+
+- active `admin` membership in the source conversation;
+- an explicit user acknowledgement naming the destination scope;
+- a structured collaboration/audit event containing identifiers, not content;
+- a transaction that records the grant before publishing the derivative.
+
+No background job, model, provider, or proposal payload may infer or request
+declassification.
+
+## Migration and legacy data
+
+1. Add nullable columns and same-tenant indexes/references without changing
+   read behavior.
+2. Create one conversation for each existing meeting and grant only its
+   verified owner an admin membership. Do not translate legacy `team` or
+   `public` values into tenant-wide grants.
+3. Link derivatives whose source meeting is deterministically recoverable.
+   Validate tenant equality at each hop.
+4. Inventory unresolved meeting-derived rows. Quarantine them from all reads
+   and injection until reconciled; do not default them to `org` or guess an
+   owner. Emit counts/IDs to an operator-only migration report, never content.
+5. Enable application dual-write of mandatory origins, switch reads to the
+   shared caller predicate, validate constraints, then require a conversation
+   ID for new meetings.
+
+Rollback leaves the new columns/tables in place while disabling the new write
+path. It must never restore legacy `team`/`public` grants or make quarantined
+rows tenant-visible.
+
+## Edge cases and denial policy
+
+- **No collaboration actor:** deny. Provisioning is a separate authenticated
+  server operation, not a read-side fallback.
+- **Actor inactive or membership inactive:** deny immediately.
+- **Caller is meeting owner but owner membership is missing:** deny and surface
+  an operator health signal; do not silently synthesize a grant on read.
+- **Unknown participant label:** display only; no grant.
+- **Multiple tenant memberships:** the authenticated request tenant selects one
+  tenant before actor resolution; no cross-tenant fallback.
+- **Conversation/meeting tenant mismatch:** reject write and deny read.
+- **Missing or deleted access origin:** deny derivative and record a reason.
+- **Membership changes during streaming:** authorize before each source fetch;
+  do not send newly fetched content after revocation. Content already sent in a
+  completed response cannot be recalled.
+- **Duplicate derivative:** keep the original access conversation; dedupe keys
+  include tenant and access origin so a restricted artifact cannot collide with
+  and inherit an org artifact.
+- **Provider spoofed participant/share/actor:** ignore for authorization.
+- **Legacy public meeting:** owner-only after migration until explicitly shared.
+- **Search timing/count side channels:** cap/batch queries and return uniform
+  empty results; no pre-authorization counts.
+
+## Rejected alternatives
+
+### Keep tenant-only authorization and filter in prompts
+
+Rejected because content would already have crossed the authorization boundary
+before prompt filtering. It also cannot stop proposal or memory laundering.
+
+### Copy user IDs or ACL lists to every derivative
+
+Rejected because revocation would require fan-out updates and partial failures
+would leave stale grants. A stable conversation origin plus read-time current
+membership is smaller and safer.
+
+### Treat participant labels, provider identities, or `team` as authority
+
+Rejected because labels are unverified, providers are transports, and the
+current `team` value has no explicit member resolver.
+
+### Use provider RLS or an AI SDK feature as the authority
+
+Rejected because it would split authority from the product-owned run and
+collaboration protocols and couple security to replaceable infrastructure.
+
+### Introduce a generic policy engine now
+
+Rejected for the MVP. Conversation membership is sufficient for the measured
+meeting-sharing requirement and is already integrated.
+
+## Security review (OWASP Top 10)
+
+- **A01 Broken Access Control:** primary threat. Enforce authenticated caller,
+  tenant, active actor, current membership, access origin, and not-found denial
+  at every repository boundary; test direct and indirect object access.
+- **A02 Cryptographic Failures:** no new secrets or sensitive values in tokens,
+  URLs, logs, or attribution rows. Existing platform encryption remains.
+- **A03 Injection:** parameterized SQL/query builders only; search strings are
+  data and never interpolated into SQL, provider instructions, or policy code.
+- **A04 Insecure Design:** fail closed, one authority graph, explicit
+  declassification, atomic provenance writes, and revocation-at-read address
+  abuse paths by design.
+- **A05 Security Misconfiguration:** database checks and application guards are
+  both required; no development bypass or permissive fallback.
+- **A06 Vulnerable Components:** no new runtime dependency is required.
+- **A07 Identification/Authentication Failures:** caller derives from existing
+  authenticated internal user context; provider IDs and request actor IDs are
+  never trusted.
+- **A08 Software/Data Integrity Failures:** composite tenant references,
+  mandatory origins, transactional writes, and immutable run evidence protect
+  provenance integrity.
+- **A09 Logging/Monitoring Failures:** record allow/deny reason codes, caller
+  actor ID, source type/ID, run ID, and policy version without content. Alert on
+  missing origins and tenant mismatches.
+- **A10 SSRF:** no new URL fetch path. Connector/media fetching remains outside
+  this issue and cannot be used as an authorization proof.
+
+## DRY and architecture boundaries
+
+- Reuse collaboration actors/memberships and the product run context.
+- Add one caller/access-origin predicate and call it from memory, knowledge,
+  proposal, meeting, and tool repositories. Do not duplicate role matrices.
+- Keep domain-specific projections: the Python meeting service may join the
+  shared collaboration authority tables but must not invent a parallel ACL.
+- Keep TanStack Query, router, Vercel AI SDK, transport, and providers outside
+  the authority layer. Cache keys may include caller/tenant, but cache contents
+  are never proof of authorization.
+- Preserve structured attribution as evidence; it is not permission.
+
+## Ambiguity policy
+
+Use the `/dev` seven-dimension decision rubric for any specification gap.
+Proceed only at 80% or greater confidence when the choice preserves every
+fail-closed boundary above, and record it in `decisions.md`. Below 80%, or for
+any choice that could widen a grant, change declassification, trust a provider,
+or expose more content, stop and return to plan review.
+
+## TDD acceptance matrix
+
+At minimum, development must first add tests for these scenarios:
+
+1. Alice and Bob are active in one tenant; only Alice belongs to the senior
+   meeting conversation. Alice can search/read the meeting and derivatives;
+   Bob receives empty search results and uniform not-found responses.
+2. Bob knows exact meeting, memory, chunk, and proposal IDs. Direct reads,
+   chain traversal, proposal actions, and knowledge/memory tools reveal no
+   content or existence.
+3. Alice creates a meeting-derived memory/proposal/chunk. Its access origin is
+   copied transactionally. Removing Alice's membership makes every subsequent
+   direct and indirect read fail without modifying the derivative.
+4. A meeting-derived write with no conversation, a mismatched tenant, an
+   inactive origin, or an unresolved participant fails; no partial derivative
+   is committed.
+5. A provider/tool payload supplies Bob's actor ID or a different tenant. The
+   server ignores/rejects it and derives Alice from the authenticated run.
+6. An unknown participant label and legacy `team`/`public` flag grant no access.
+7. A restricted proposal cannot create an org-visible work item through normal
+   acceptance. Explicit admin declassification succeeds once and records an
+   audit event.
+8. Existing caller-owned private memory and true org memory remain visible only
+   in their intended lanes; tenant-only direct memory APIs no longer expose a
+   different user's private memory.
+9. A cross-tenant actor, conversation, or derivative reference is rejected by
+   both application validation and database constraints.
+10. A denied search emits privacy-safe telemetry and no model-visible count,
+    title, summary, embedding text, participant, action item, or rationale.
+
+## Baseline and conflict evidence
+
